  "abstract": "<jats:p>\n                    We demonstrate a thin-film lithium tantalate (TFLT) Mach-Zehnder modulator with balanced bandwidth-voltage and low DC drift. Precise waveguide geometry optimization yields a 3-dB bandwidth (BW)\u2009&gt;\u200967\u2005GHz and a half-wave voltage-length product (\n                    <jats:italic>V</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sub>\u03c0</jats:sub>\n                    <jats:italic>L</jats:italic>\n                    ) of 2.4\u2005V\u00b7cm, resulting in a higher BW/\n                    <jats:italic>V</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sub>\u03c0</jats:sub>\n                    <jats:sup>2</jats:sup>\n                    compared to the previously reported TFLT modulators. The device also shows excellent operational stability, with its\n                    <jats:italic>V</jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sub>\u03c0</jats:sub>\n                    remaining stable across a wide range of driving frequencies and power drift below 1\u2005dB over 60 minutes. Furthermore, we developwhat we believe is a novel and stable measurement technique which employs a cascaded dual-ring modulation structure to quantitatively assess the DC drift, confirming the long-term viability of TFLT modulators for high-speed, stable, and low-power integrated photonics.\n                  </jats:p>",
